                    === long stuff ===

Data hoarding has been my primary obsession the since
early 2024, not barring how debiliating it is when I
lose something.  Before this was officially a thing,
I backed up my Perspective diaries after losing a few
entries to iCloud sync.  The code isn't available
not because I don't believe open source but because
I deemed it to be "too personal" for GitHub.  (Yes,
you'll probably find this inconceivable, but code and
git are my continued diary; but if you try to believe
it, you'll see just how important it is to get people
who actually care to read it.)

When I discovered that Git (with git-annex) was a good
idea for tracking terabytes of files associated with
provenance information (fancy word for "where-stuff-
comes-from", which is just git log).  Thus began the
grand unofficial "archive project", a race to back up
everything I've made offline...

That has mostly been a success, but with a few issues.
BIG issues that undermine the whole thing, really.

  * Backups are dead files.  You can't interact with
    them (or at least, not in a plesant way; like if you
    long for the convenient interface of Google Drive or
    social media... well you don't have any of that. :D)

  * Backups are not verifiable.  You won't get a checksum
    of every tweet you made on Twitter; a checksum of every
    photo on Google Photos.  You won't get a checksum at
    all with Google Takeout! (save from the massive archive-
    browser.html...)  Because of this I only trust something
    when I can verify it visually against the user interface
    of the host service.  That means (at minimum) a faithful
    recreation of the interface is necessary.

(These have been the same problems I had been trying to
address with Project Perspective, but that was more of
a niche thing.  I hand-copied and formatted those into
JSON entries that you wouldn't get from the actual app.)

So the "steps" (not sure how to call this) of an ideal
process to data reclaim I figured are as follows.

  * Acquisition:  When and where did I obtain this thing?
    (URL) What lens did I observe this thing through?
    (Protocol)  Is it complete?  Is it the "original"
    and not a derived copy? (Canonicalization)

  * Structure:  Is it encoded in Apple bplist, msgpack, or
    Google Protobuf?  Is it a float or an int?  Big endian
    or little endian?  Mostly a problem with proprietary
    formats only.  (JSON, YAML, EDN/S-expr don't have this
    issue, as they are self-documenting.)

  * Interpretation:  What are the semantic meanings of the
    fields parsed according to the schema?  Does it have a
    canonical form?  How certain are we that this is correct?

  * Representation:  How to load all this stuff into memory?
    (Parsing)  What states to store, with which data type?
    What operations are useful for retrieving information
    from said states?  (Interface for programmers/API)

  * View/Presentation:  The interface with prettier stuff.

Acquisition is assumed because archive-bridge is strictly
an analytic tool of static files -- dead stuff that doesn't
change.  (By proxy, archive-bridge runs completely offline.)

Presentation is a non-goal of archive-bridge, because
it is in my belief an intrinsically *opinionated* step.
Some people want a static-site generator (11ty Twitter),
others want a REST API (i.e. make everything JSON), and
non-techy people like my family just want to install an
app and call it a day.  Then there's freaks like me who
are obsessed with S-expressions to death and, like the
Rust rewrite craze, want to turn all the data into one.

Even if we take a step back and make a compromise and say,
"OK, let's all just use JSON."  We still lose possibly
the most important thing: policy and mechanism.  You can't
choose what to do with faulty/inconsistent data, whether
to preserve the exact value or use lossy floating-point
arithmetic.  And serialization formats like JSON may be
self-documenting, but they are not self-runnable --- it's
not *programmable*, I guess you could say.  And you lose
the mechanism that operates on the data, which is no less
important than the data themselves to me.  (A JSON file
or a static website with no code to interpret it is just
as dead as the source files they were generated from.  It
is my belief that they cannot stand on its own, no matter
how good you are at naming the fields, if you cannot run
it and observe its behavior as it (in)validates itself.)

There is a line of Unix philosphy that is, in ESR's words,
"Separate policy from mechanism; separate interfaces from
engine".  You provide the input, archive-bridge provides
the engine, so that you or someone else can provide the
code on top of archive-bridge that outputs JSON, HTML, or
displays it as an archive viewer app... :)

                    === languages ===

The archive-bridge library itself will be developed
uniformly in Java 17 (using the gradle build system),
though you should expect to see other languages used
for experimentation and prototyping.  (Perl Template
Toolkit namely is used in the apple-notes bridge to
generate boilerplate accessors, though you do not need
to know either to understand the code it generates!)

